# schedule/ROSTER -- the single source scheduler#79 is building toward.
# project | account@host | rate | state
#
# NOT YET AUTHORITATIVE. schedule/_paced.monkey.conf, schedule/FREEZE, and
# each schedule/<project>.conf are still what dispatch actually reads today;
# this file is a parallel derivation of the same set, kept honest by
# `bash bin/roster-diff.sh`. It becomes authoritative only when the
# retirement PR (hf7y/scheduler#79) removes those three as liveness sources
# -- bin/roster-target.sh's `onekey` probe is what measures that landing,
# not this file merely existing with rows in it (`oneroster`).
#
# ONLY ZACH EDITS THIS FILE. schedule/FREEZE's header reserves its
# human-adds-the-line guard for exactly this reason ("a row added ... by an
# agent ... still dispatches NOTHING until a human adds a line here"). Once
# #80's `dose <project>` reads this file to converge a host, editing a row
# here IS arming or parking a project -- the same act FREEZE will not let an
# agent do to itself. Until then this file is inert prose an agent may read
# but must not change, and least of all change-then-converge against.
#
# `state` is ONE field (live/parked) -- the whole point, per #79: it is what
# makes "enabled=1 in one file but EXEMPT commented in another" unrepresentable.
# `rate` was 6h for every row -- the value the migration inherited from
# _runner.monkey.conf so that it changed nothing. RAISED TO 2h for the live
# rows on 2026-08-12, Zach-directed ("up the arithmetic to increase pace").
#
# THE ARITHMETIC, so the next reader can redo it rather than trust it.
# 5 live accounts, PACED_MAX_PER_TICK=1, and each account can only execute its
# OWN row (the other homes are 0700), so a tick is one dispatch per account:
#
#   6h  ->  4 ticks/day x 5 =  20 dispatches/day   (before)
#   2h  -> 12 ticks/day x 5 =  60 dispatches/day   (now, 3x)
#
# Note PACED_MAX_PER_TICK is NOT a lever here: it is capped by the number of
# rows an account can actually run, which is one. Under host dispatch
# (hf7y/scheduler#55) it becomes a real knob; today only the interval is.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE ONLY PLACE THE RATE IS SET. _runner.<host>.conf's RUNNER_CRON
# is deliberately left alone: it is the legacy writer, applied by running
# `sync-crontab.sh --apply` AS EACH ACCOUNT, which is the five-way manual dance
# hf7y/scheduler#81 exists to retire. Editing both would have made two writers
# for one fact, and whichever ran last would win silently -- exactly
# hf7y/scheduler#119. Change the rate HERE and converge with the verb:
#
#     dose <project> --apply        (on the host named in the row)
#
# WHAT THE GATE SAID WHEN THIS WAS SET, measured not assumed:
#   verdict=RUN binding=7d util=0.310 burnline=0.258 slack=-0.052
#   resets_in_min=7482
# 31% of the 7-day window used with 1.8 days elapsed -- ALREADY ABOVE the
# even-burn line, and running only because USAGE_RUSH_BEFORE_RESET_MIN=10080
# disables pacing for the whole window. At 3x, the 0.99 ceiling is reachable
# in roughly a day, after which the gate HOLDS until the window rolls. That is
# the ceiling working as configured (Zach, 2026-07-29), not a failure -- but it
# means the observable outcome of this change may be "burns hard, then pauses"
# rather than "burns evenly". Dial the rate here if that is not wanted.
#
# Derived 2026-08-11 from _paced.monkey.conf (enabled==1), FREEZE (uncommented
# EXEMPT: <p>@monkey), and each schedule/<p>.conf's CRON_HOST/CRON_ACCOUNT --
# live only where all three agree. Re-verify, don't trust this comment:
#   bash bin/roster-diff.sh

